<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=theory-of-relativity">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theory of relativity</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1905">1905</a>–<a href="/years/1915">1915</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The theory of relativity comprises two theories of physics by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a>: special relativity, published in 1905, and general relativity, a theory of gravitation he developed between 1907 and 1915. Special relativity, introduced in the paper &quot;On the Electrodynamics of Moving Bodies&quot;, holds that light in a vacuum has the same speed for all observers and that mass and energy are equivalent (E = mc²). General relativity explains gravity through curved spacetime. The theories superseded Newton's mechanics, and satellite navigation systems such as GPS must allow for their effects.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theory-of-relativity.jpg" alt="Theory of relativity" width="320" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frank Watson Dyson / Arthur Eddington / Charles Rundle Davidson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1919_eclipse_positive.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1905</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Special relativity was published in 1905, general relativity, a theory of gravitation, in 1915.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Laws of motion and gravitation<span>1687</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/electromagnetic-induction">Electromagnetic induction<span>1831</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=theory-of-relativity">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
